1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,010
Listen to a talk in an anthropology class.

2
00:00:04,010 --> 00:00:10,442
Previously, we discussed the significance of pottery to ancient cultures, including pottery as an art form

3
00:00:10,442 --> 00:00:12,170
and pottery's crucial role in trade.

4
00:00:12,170 --> 00:00:15,230
Today, we'll focus on the development of pottery over time.

5
00:00:15,230 --> 00:00:22,170
One breakthrough was the invention of the potter's wheel around 3500 BCE in Mesopotamia.

6
00:00:22,170 --> 00:00:25,911
Before this, pottery was shaped mostly by hand,

7
00:00:25,911 --> 00:00:29,150
which was slow and produced less uniform results.

8
00:00:29,150 --> 00:00:33,810
The potter's wheel allowed artisans to spin and shape the clay more quickly

9
00:00:33,810 --> 00:00:38,290
and evenly, making it possible to create symmetrical vessels with greater precision and speed.

10
00:00:38,290 --> 00:00:40,901
Firing technology also advanced.

11
00:00:40,901 --> 00:00:43,392
Early pots were fired in open pits,

12
00:00:43,392 --> 00:00:48,030
which limited temperature control, resulting in uneven or fragile pots.

13
00:00:48,030 --> 00:00:49,939
So kilns were developed:

14
00:00:49,939 --> 00:00:54,710
enclosed ovens that could reach higher and more consistent temperatures.

15
00:00:54,710 --> 00:00:58,050
These allowed for stronger, more durable pottery.

16
00:00:58,050 --> 00:01:00,413
Another advance was the updraft kiln,

17
00:01:00,413 --> 00:01:03,170
which directed heat upward through the chamber.

18
00:01:03,170 --> 00:01:07,616
By adjusting the airflow, potters could control how much oxygen reached the fire.

19
00:01:07,616 --> 00:01:11,036
This helped them produce pottery with specific colors, like red

20
00:01:11,036 --> 00:01:15,140
or black, depending on how the clay reacted to the firing atmosphere.

21
00:01:15,140 --> 00:01:21,114
Then, in ancient China, potters developed kilns that could reach very high temperatures.

22
00:01:21,114 --> 00:01:25,250
This allowed them to create stoneware and eventually porcelain.

23
00:01:25,250 --> 00:01:30,290
Porcelain is made from a special mix of fine clay and minerals

24
00:01:30,290 --> 00:01:34,070
and must be fired at temperatures above 1200°C.

25
00:01:34,070 --> 00:01:40,230
The result is a smooth, white ceramic that is strong, lightweight, and slightly translucent.

26
00:01:40,230 --> 00:01:46,203
Porcelain was a major breakthrough because it was more durable

27
00:01:46,203 --> 00:01:49,190
and versatile than earlier pottery.
